// THE UNIFIED RIDE in a tail-loop spine (Eric 2026-08-29, "crazy in,
// crazy out"). The outer select is the tail-loop spine; its scrutinee is
// the INNER select, which bottoms at the base case (`1 % 0` = bottom at
// n=0). On the interp the spine holds its `_` selection (built at n=3,2,1)
// and, on the bottom scrutinee, RUNS the held arm — the recursive jump —
// so the loop CONTINUES to n=-1, where `1 % -1 = 0` matches the base arm
// and emits acc=0. The kernel tail spine kept no per-iteration selection,
// so it bottomed out at n=0 and emitted nothing (interp [0] vs jit []).
// Fix: the tail spine de-fuses when its scrutinee can bottom mid-loop
// (`scrut_cannot_ride`) — a nested select / arith / call scrutinee — so
// the node-walk runs the ride. A bare-formal scrutinee never rides (the
// formal rides its previous value) and stays fused (the 120-185x family
// is untouched). aug28b hz0 divergence_000000.
{let n = i64:10; let rec ap = |f: fn(x: i64) -> i64, n: i64, acc: i64| -> i64 select select n {m if m <= i64:0 => i64:1 % m, m => f(m)} {i64:0 => acc, _ => ap(f, n - i64:1, acc)}; ap(|x| n, i64:3, i64:0)}
